course: dictionary
lesson: 4920
title: Words that last for ever
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Immortal means never dying, living for ever. Look at its parts: im- means not, and mortal means able to die. So immortal is a word for something that cannot die.
explain: «Dried tardigrades survive great cold, high pressure and radiation, but they are not immortal, and active tardigrades are quite fragile.» The word not tells you the writer is saying something important. Surviving hard conditions is not the same as never dying.
explain: «The immortal jellyfish can turn back into a young polyp when it is hurt or old, so in theory it could go on forever, though most are eaten.» The words though most are eaten show that it is not truly immortal in real life. Context clues often hold the answer.
explain: Immortal can also mean remembered for ever, as in «the immortal words» of a famous speech. Read the sentence around it to decide which meaning fits.
fact: Immortal means never dying, or living for ever.
fact: Immortal can also mean remembered for ever, like «the immortal words» of a great writer.
fact: In myths, gods and magical creatures are often called immortal.
fact: Tiny tardigrades can survive great cold, but they are not immortal.
fact: The jellyfish Turritopsis can turn back into a young stage, but it can still be eaten or fall ill, so it is not truly immortal.
exercise: Gods in myths are often called immortal. What does immortal mean? | Very tall | *Never dying | Made of stone | Always hungry
exercise: «Dried tardigrades survive great cold, but they are not immortal.» Why does the writer say they are not immortal? | *They can still die | They live in cold places | They are very small | They are famous
exercise: Priya says «the immortal words» means words that were lost. What does it really mean? | Words that are sung loudly | *Words that will be remembered for ever | Words that are quiet | Words that were never written
test: «Though most are eaten, the immortal jellyfish could go on forever in theory.» What does in theory mean? | *As a possibility on paper, not always true in real life | Definitely, every time | Slowly | In a dream
test: The word immortal is made of im- (not) and mortal (able to die). What does this tell you? | It means very small | It means able to fly | *It means not able to die | It means brave
exam: Which sentence uses immortal to mean remembered for ever? | «The immortal rock was cold and grey.» | *«Her immortal song is still sung today.» | «The immortal dog ran home.»
